<<~ ahu slot=`#/head`>>

! Evaluators ~ one engine, one suite, many hosts

''The fork it closes.'' The portable-engine talk story left one question standing: should the house run ''one evaluator'' of its wikitext and filter dialects everywhere, or let ''many evaluators'' grow, one per host? The operator's bet frames the answer: ''the language carries portability.'' This meme reads that bet against twenty-odd languages that tried both roads, and lands one ruling.

''The question dissolves.'' Prior art never decides "one or many" first. It decides ''whether the language owns a conformance suite any evaluator can face.'' Every dialect found that lives honestly with more than one evaluator owns such a suite; every dialect that drifted lacked one, kept it optional, or shaped it after one implementation. The count of evaluators then follows from host need, and the suite decides whether a second one may exist honestly.

''So the bet sharpens:'' the language carries portability, and ''its suite carries the language.''

''Register.'' House prose, rewritten from a tasked Fable spirit's web survey of 2026-10-16. Source marks travel as the spirit gave them: //fetched// (read on GitHub), //search// (a search passage), //inferred// (reasoning, flagged). The spirit read the three language specs, the tree-sitter package and two sibling reports; it edited nothing and booted nothing.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-ruling`>>

!! The ruling, four strokes

# ''One reference evaluator for the full dialects: the TW5 engine in JS,'' running wherever an engine can run ~ browsers, Node, QuickJS, Hermes, a UE6 sidecar's embedded JS. This keeps the standing frame ENGINE = TW5 VM. Lean hosts (Mudlet, MCP, chat) receive parses, results and blocks over `port:ask`; the language never travels to them.
# ''Build the conformance suite now, evaluator-neutral, with the pinned JS engine as oracle'' ~ the sqllogictest move: run the reference over a frozen world, record, review once by eye, freeze, and diff from then on. The house filter dialect (`wikitext-filter`) names no suite today; this fills that hole first.
# ''The parse tree carries normative force; each camera's serialisation stands second,'' with tolerance declared per test and degradation cases mandatory. TW5 already tests at the tree (`rule`, `start`, `end` on every node), and the house spec already binds normative force to parsing.
# ''A certified subset evaluator stays permitted, never scheduled.'' A lean host that proves an offline need may grow one, green against the suite's `core` section, publishing its skip list by name. Nothing in the forward attractors asks for it today.

''Why build a suite with only one evaluator?'' Because one evaluator removes ''language'' drift and leaves ''host'' drift. The same engine on V8 and on Hermes parts ways wherever `Intl`, `localeCompare`, Unicode regular expressions or `Date` differ. test262 exists for exactly this reason: one spec, many engines claiming it. A suite run per host names that drift before an operator meets it. This alone justifies the suite even if the house never writes a second evaluator.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-record`>>

!! What the record shows

''TiddlyWiki's own record.'' In roughly twenty years nobody ported TW5's filter language or wikitext parser to another language and kept it in step. TiddlyWeb's Python filters, "borrowed from filterTiddlers … does not check its own syntax", became their own dialect (//fetched//). WikklyText rendered TiddlyWiki Classic only (//search//). Two Rust converters of 2025–26 translate easy constructs to Markdown and copy the rest through, named (//fetched//). Upstream's MultiWikiServer plan expects "a query syntax supporting a subset of filter operators" server-side (//search//). Each attempt ended as a subset with a divergence list, and TW5's own corpus could not certify any of them: about 75 filter cases comparing comma-joined strings, about 25 parse-tree cases, nine render cases (//fetched//).

''Languages that hold many evaluators,'' each with its suite: CommonMark (500+ examples in `spec.txt`, `spec_tests.py --program`), JSONPath RFC 9535 (`cts.json`, `results` for allowed non-determinism), JMESPath (`given`/`expression`/`result`/`error`), Liquid via golden-liquid (`result`/`results`, `invalid`, tags such as `utc` and `error string`), Mustache (optional `~` modules, "Mustache-like" for partial implementations), Dhall, CEL (parse, check, eval phases; `-skip_tests` at name boundaries), test262 (`negative`, `features`, `flags`), WebAssembly (`.wast` with a reference interpreter as oracle), W3C QT3, WPT (reftests with declared `fuzzy`), sqllogictest (expectations recorded from already-correct engines, 7.2 million queries at one count).

''Languages that drifted,'' each where the suite went missing or bent to one implementation: Markdown before CommonMark (fifteen renderings of one paragraph); Jinja against Nunjucks (`true` against `True`, native methods); GraphQL beyond graphql-js (divergence never measured); Datalog dialects (differential testing could not even be set up); Lua across PUC, LuaJIT and Fengari (iteration order, `\x`, integers, strings); CUE (tests "reflect properties of the current implementation"); Nix until Tvix wrote `nix_oracle` against C++ Nix 2.3; ink, whose ports broke on version lag rather than wrong code.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/golden-principles`>>

!! Golden principles

# ''The examples form the suite, and the suite forms the spec's executable half.'' CommonMark and Dhall hold implementations to every case; a failure names an implementation bug, an underspecified standard or a bad test. `tiddlywiki-wikitext` already points at `editions/test`; the corpus behind that pointer runs thin.
# ''A reference implementation without a shared suite leaves divergence unmeasured,'' and a suite shaped like one implementation certifies only that implementation (graphql-js, CUE).
# ''A multi-evaluator language publishes its divergence list by name'' ~ liquidjs, gojq, jaq, JMESPath Community, Handlebars. The house's own refuse-by-name law, applied to a language.
# ''Ports drift first at numbers, strings, ordering and truthiness.'' TW5 filters compare as strings except where `nsort` and the math operators coerce; the suite carries those cases first.
# ''Non-determinism lives in the suite's format,'' never in an implementer's judgement: `results` lists for open orderings, a `set` tag for unordered comparison, a `utc`-style tag where the host clock matters.
# ''Feature tags let an evaluator stand honestly partial'' ~ Mustache modules, test262 `features`, Lua's `_port`, Saxon's 415 excluded tests, regorus naming its 23 failing suites.
# ''Errors and degradation count as conformance.'' TW5's filter tests barely touch errors ("Just don't get RSoD"; "this one is strange"; "????"). The house's total-parse law makes degradation the normal path, so the suite tests it as such.
# ''Ports track the reference by version; lag fails them, not wrong code.'' Every suite run pins a TW5 commit, as the spec already pins its description to `69b98fc94`.
# ''Ship the tree when the consumer only renders; embed the evaluator when the round trip dominates.'' Pandoc's JSON AST for filters in any language, then embedded Lua when the pipe cost bit; ESTree as lingua franca across Acorn, Babel and Esprima.
# ''Differential testing needs a controlled oracle,'' or it tests dialects instead of engines (the Datalog study; SQuaLity's cross-suite runs).
# ''Where host constructs leak into a language, portability dies.'' TW5 leaks JS at `regexp`, at sort comparison, at the `jsonget` family and through widget variables. The house dialect already fenced `!!field` and ambient `currentTiddler` (→ `[self[]]`, explicit context); the suite fences the rest with a `host-bound` tag.
# ''The suite's grain matches the contract's grain.'' Filters compare at the result set; wikitext compares at the parse tree, then at each camera.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-suite`>>

!! The suite's shape

!!! Filters (`filter-cts`)

One JSON file per section, assembled by script into one `filter-cts.json`, never hand-edited at the top. A test carries:

```json
{
  "name":       "toml/key-equals/string",
  "filter":     "[toml:register[SC]sort[toml:title]]",
  "world":      "worlds/pono-small",
  "context":    { "self": "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/meme" },
  "result":     ["lar:///…", "lar:///…"],
  "results":    null,
  "parse":      { "runs": [] },
  "fault":      null,
  "tags":       ["core", "toml", "order-sensitive", "reviewed"],
  "since":      "wikitext-filter 0.1 / tw5 69b98fc94",
  "host-bound": false
}
```

* ''Oracle first, eye second.'' The pinned engine writes `result`; a reviewer reads each once and tags it `reviewed`. A doubted case keeps a `strange` tag rather than vanishing.
* ''Sections follow the dialect's own tables:'' `preserved` (TW5 operators kept), `added` (`toml:`, `stack:`, `self[]`, `ahu:id`, `edge:family`), `deprecated` (diagnostic ''and'' result), `fault` (unknown operator, unbalanced brackets, empty operand), `context` (every scope row ~ carrier `#meta`, `meme` block, `huli` variable, `wehe`/`helu` parameter).
* ''Order gets declared per test.'' Where the dialect promises only a set, the test says so.
* ''Host-bound operators carry the tag;'' an evaluator's skip list names them at name boundaries, and a skipped case still parses and still degrades.
* ''Degradation forms a section.'' Each fault states the diagnostic and the result returned while continuing; an evaluator that throws fails.
* ''Versioning'' pins the dialect version and the oracle's TW5 commit. A changed expectation lands only with a `since` bump and a ledger line, under the `host-ts/parity.mjs` discipline: a drifting hash names a disagreement, never a content edit.
* ''Start near 250 tests'' (//inferred// from the operator tables): each operator plain, negated and empty-operand; each context row twice; each fault once. Growth follows bugs.

!!! Wikitext, per camera

''Layer 1, the parse tree (normative).'' Input text → tree, every node with `type`, `rule`, `start`, `end`, plus the graph-layer additions (sigil name, args, pairing, pranala edges). The carrier grammar's specimens and pinned structural hashes already run this way across two hosts; the same manifest grows upward into the TW5 inline layer. Degradation cases stay mandatory: a stray `</br>` kept as text, an unclosed `<div>` still an element, a stray `<<` left as text.

''Layer 2, cameras (per target, tolerance declared).'' One golden per specimen and camera:

* `html` ~ exact after a declared normaliser (attribute order, class whitespace), so TW5's whitespace sensitivity becomes a rule rather than a trap.
* `text` ~ exact.
* `blocks` ~ a JSON tree of heading, paragraph, list, link, field and `affordances[]`; exact on structure, affordance ids compared by position since ids live one render.
* `label-html` (Mudlet's Qt subset) and any later canvas camera ~ checked against the same `blocks` golden through a declared projection, never a hand-written golden of their own (WPT's rule that a reference avoids the technology under test, applied to cameras).
* ''A missing camera tests too:'' the spec says a renderer MUST degrade a missing camera visibly, and the suite records that look per camera.
* `fuzzy` ~ only where a camera loses detail by nature (a 3D layout), bounded in difference and count, never "roughly".

''Fixtures.'' TW5's 274 test tiddlers under `editions/test/data/` form the base; the house adds its `authored-*` and `wild-*` specimens. Filter tests and render tests share one frozen world, so a transclusion resolves against the same titles a filter selects.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-hybrid`>>

!! The hybrid that fits, in landing order

# ''The reference stays the TW5 engine in JS,'' embedded where a host allows. Filters ride `port:ask{kind:"filter"}`; renders ride `port:ask{kind:"render", target}`. Results and trees travel; the language stays home.
# ''The suite travels with the dialect as a submodule-grade artifact'' ~ evaluator-neutral JSON, generated, reviewed, pinned, tagged. It runs from day one on three hosts: the browser engine, Node, and each embedded JS engine the house targets, each with a skip list by name. This turns "the language carries portability" from a bet into a measurement: the contract reads as `filter-cts.json` plus the specimen manifests, and a host's conformance reads as a published row.
# ''A certified subset evaluator, permitted, not scheduled.'' Its charter: the `core` section only (selection, tags, TOML keys, stacks, `self[]`, string `sort`, `limit`/`first`/`last`/`reverse`, Boolean steps), explicit context, no widget variables, no `regexp`, no math, nothing host-bound. Green on `core` earns the name `wikitext-filter, core`; anything less calls itself "filter-like". It evaluates against the suite's own frozen-world shape, a snapshot a lean host may already hold. A lean host that wants the carrier parse takes it from tree-sitter's per-host bindings (web-tree-sitter on WASM hosts; `ltreesitter` where Lua can `dlopen`), never from a second hand-written parser.

''What the hybrid refuses:'' a second full evaluator of TW5 wikitext or filters in any language. Every record says that road ends in a subset with a divergence list, maintained forever against an upstream that moves every few months.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/host-landing`>>

!! Landing on the forward attractors

|!Host |!Evaluator |!What travels |!Suite role |
|Browsers |reference engine |records; the glass renders itself |full suite in CI; `html` goldens |
|Node |reference engine |records, html, text, blocks |full suite; the oracle run that writes goldens happens here |
|QuickJS / quickjs-ng |reference engine (ES2020, no WASM, pump-mode timers) |as Node |full suite per build; expected skips touch `Intl` |
|Hermes |reference engine (Proxy, Intl, local eval historically thin) |as Node |full suite per build; `host-bound` earns its keep here first (//inferred//) |
|Mudlet (Lua 5.1, no JS, no WASM) |''none'' ~ the lararium island evaluates |`text`, `label-html`, `blocks`; invoke by affordance id |`label-html` golden derived from `blocks`; a `core` subset in Lua only on a named offline need |
|UE6 sidecar |reference engine in the sidecar's JS; Verse receives blocks |blocks, text; writes as records |sidecar build runs the suite; a 3D camera holds the one rightful `fuzzy` |
|MCP |consumer |results, text, html |the `fault` section supplies the filter tool's error vocabulary |

''Refinement over the spirit's table.'' The template insight of the floor (any root template or widget tree serves as a render target; the page DOM stands as Jeremy's most pono browser projection) lands here as one rule: ''a camera names a root template plus a serialiser,'' and the suite's camera layer keys its goldens by that pair. The browser's page template and a Mudlet pane's block template then differ only in root and serialiser, never in engine, and one suite holds both honest. The `blocks` camera waits on the Annex B widget-tree pass of `memetic-wikitext`; until it lands, the camera layer starts at `html` and `text`.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/mu`>>

!! Mu

* ''Wand `*` (visible, ignition).'' The record runs unanimous: every honest multi-evaluator language owns a suite with results, error names, feature tags and a version pin. TW5 owns about 110 Jasmine cases and no second evaluator in two decades; the house filter dialect owns none. Ignite the suite, not a port.
* ''Cup `?` (macro, sympathy).'' The operator's bet and the engine ruling need not fight. The single JS engine becomes the contract's first signatory, not its owner, and the suite keeps the door open for a host that cannot run JS without forcing anyone through it.
* ''Sword `!` (micro, discernment).'' A second evaluator would drift at `regexp`, sort comparison, `nsort` coercion, widget-variable context and every plugin operator. Name them `host-bound`; keep them out of any subset charter.
* ''Pentacle `_` (hidden, ground).'' Who re-pours the goldens when upstream TW5 moves? Pinning makes the upgrade a visible diff; without an owner for that diff the suite rots, as W3C QT3 nearly did after its working group closed.
* ''Arcana `0` (unask).'' "One or many" dissolves into "is there a suite, and who serves as its oracle." Yes; the JS engine, pinned.
* ''Dubious-Move `?!`, carried.'' Micro certainty says no forward attractor needs a second evaluator; macro relation says the portability bet wants the possibility alive. Resolve by building the suite now and second implementations never, until a host earns one.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/two-stories`>>

!! Two stories

''Gaia, 2026.'' An operator reads one quest journal three ways: a browser tab, a Mudlet pane fed over GMCP, a phone shell whose JS runs on Hermes. One evening the phone's "recent quests" list comes back in another order: the filter sorts by locale, the phone engine lacks `Intl`, and "Ängsbacka" sinks to the bottom (//inferred//: a plausible drift, not a measured one). No second evaluator exists anywhere in the chain; the drift came from the host. A `host-bound` sort test with Hermes on its skip list would have named the difference first. The Mudlet pane showed the browser's order, because it received a result, never a language.

''Elyncia, 5492.'' Two spirits keep one crossroads shrine. The elder holds the whole engine in orichalcum and answers every filter the district asks. The younger, a reflex spirit bound into a Lua tablet, may speak only the core rite. Before it speaks aloud it pours the libation suite, two hundred and some cups, each answer sealed in the rim; it skips the regexp cups and the number cups and says so by name at the gate, and the roll records it as "wikitext-filter, core only." When the elder gets re-cast at a new upstream commit, the rims get re-poured and diffed; three answers change, each with a ledger line. No traveller ever sees the two disagree, because the younger answers only questions the suite says both answer alike. Drift's absence looks like this: not two identical evaluators, but one contract small enough to keep, and a gate that names what each spirit may not say.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/open-rulings`>>

!! Rulings standing open

# ''Suite strength.'' `tiddlywiki-wikitext` says a parser SHOULD pass its suite; raise to MUST for the house dialects and for any non-reference evaluator, with a published skip list.
# ''Suite format.'' Adopt the `filter-cts` shape above (generated, then reviewed), or hand-write in the CommonMark manner at many times the cost.
# ''The `core` charter'' — ruled 2026-10-20: the drafted operators, with string `sort` by code point, locale-free; a locale-aware sort carries `host-bound` (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/dreamdom`>> #/open-rulings).
# ''Oracle ownership'' — ruled 2026-10-20: an engine-commit bump runs as its own lane that re-pours; a diff that changes a result or a parse tree lands as a hoʻike the operator reads.
# ''Camera tolerance.'' Exact for `html` and `text`; `blocks`-derived for `label-html`; `fuzzy` for 3D only.
# ''Mudlet offline need.'' Mudlet receives results only; reopen the subset evaluator on a named offline use.
# ''Annex B dependency.'' Start the camera layer at `html`/`text` until the widget-tree pass lands.

<<~/ahu>>

